<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saipan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saipan is the largest island and seat of government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-mariana-islands">Northern Mariana Islands</a>, a US commonwealth in the western Pacific. Under Japanese rule from 1914 it was developed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane with many settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and Okinawa. In June and July 1944 US forces captured it in one of the bloodiest battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>, in which thousands of Japanese civilians died, many by suicide, and it became a base for bombing Japan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saipan.jpg" alt="Saipan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MICHAELeDILLEY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saipan_at_Night.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Saipan lies about 190 kilometres north of Guam and 9.3 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinian">Tinian</a>, from which the Saipan Channel separates it. It is about 19 kilometres long and 9 kilometres wide, with a land area of 115.38 square kilometres. The western side is lined with sandy beaches and an offshore coral reef that creates a large lagoon, while the eastern shore is mostly rugged rocky cliffs. Archaeologists have found traces of human settlement over 4,000 years old, including petroglyphs and latte stones. Saipan is one of the main homes of the Chamorro and the Carolinians, the indigenous people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Mariana Islands</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Spanish rule began in the 17th century, and the island was possibly first sighted by Europeans during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a>'s expedition, which landed in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a> on 6 March 1521. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> of 1898 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> occupied Saipan, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> sold it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> under the German–Spanish Treaty of 1899. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> administered it as part of German New Guinea and made no attempt to develop or settle it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> captured the island in 1914 and was awarded formal control in 1919 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> as part of the South Seas Mandate. From the 1920s settlers from Japan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and Okinawa developed large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-seas-development-company">South Seas Development Company</a> built refineries, ports, roads and schools. By October 1943 there were 29,348 Japanese settlers and 3,926 Chamorro and Carolinian islanders.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> treated Saipan as part of the last line of defence of the homeland and garrisoned it heavily, with nearly 30,000 troops by mid-1944. The Battle of Saipan lasted from 15 June to 9 July 1944; American forces landed on the south-western beaches and took the island after more than three weeks of heavy fighting, at a cost of 3,426 killed and 10,364 wounded. After the war Saipan became part of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The Northern Mariana islanders voted to join the United States in 1975, and the island became a municipality of the Commonwealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-mariana-islands">Northern Mariana Islands</a> in 1978. Islanders became United States citizens on 4 November 1986.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>About 90 per cent of the population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-mariana-islands">Northern Mariana Islands</a> lives on Saipan, and since the whole island is one municipality most publications call it the Commonwealth's capital. The legislative and executive branches of government are in the village of Capitol Hill and the judicial branch in Susupe. Most of the native Chamorro and Carolinian population is Roman Catholic. Sites from the past include latte stones, the Last Command Post of the Battle of Saipan and a Japanese lighthouse built in 1934.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saipan, Northern Mariana Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1909 the orator chief Lauaki Namulauʻulu Mamoe led the Mau a Pule protest and was exiled to <b>Saipan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-samoa">German Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan developed sugar plantations on <b>Saipan</b> and Tinian through the South Seas Development Company, phosphate mining and fisheries.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-seas-mandate">South Seas Mandate</a></li><li><blockquote>The US Navy governed until 1951, then the Department of the Interior, with headquarters moved to <b>Saipan</b> in 1962.</blockquote><a href="/state/trust-territory-of-the-pacific-islands">Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands</a></li></ul></section>
